  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nStrong easterly winds are expected to develop across southern and\ncentral Arizona by Friday morning with guidance showing H8 winds\nof 30-45 kts across southern and much of central Arizona. Latest\nNAEFS and ENS show H8 winds reaching near record magnitudes \ncompared to climatology. The best corridor for strong winds looks \nto be across southeast Arizona into southern Gila County and \nthrough Pinal, southern Maricopa, and Yuma Counties. Occasional \ngusty winds of 25-40 mph are expected before sunrise in this area \nbefore increasing to advisory level around or just after sunrise \nFriday morning. Wind Advisories have been issued for the \naforementioned areas for Friday morning into the afternoon with \nwind gusts in excess of 40 mph at times. The peak of the winds \nshould occur mid to late morning Friday with some gusts up to\n50-55 mph possible. Areas to the north, including Phoenix, should\nstay below advisory level but gusts of 30-40 mph will be likely \nduring the latter half of the morning Friday. Patchy blowing dust \nwill also be a concern on Friday, particularly across Pinal \nCounty. Winds should eventually drop off some for most areas \nduring the late afternoon hours Friday, but advisory levels winds\nshould continue to be a concern through the rest of Friday and \neven into Saturday across higher terrain areas east and southeast \nof Phoenix.\n\nWinds Saturday will again be elevated with gusts of 25-35 mph for\nmuch of southern and central Arizona, but any advisory level \nwinds are likely to be limited southeast Arizona. Aside from the\nwind concern, guidance is suggesting some lower end shower\npotential later Friday through Saturday. The placement of the\ncut-off low to our southwest starting Friday is expected to advect\na good amount of mid and upper level moisture into the area. Some\nhigh-based light showers may become possible across southern\nArizona as early as Friday afternoon, but little if any\nmeasurable rainfall is expected. These low-end (10-20%) light \nshower chances may stick around through Saturday as the cut-off \nlow begins to drift back through our region, but most locations \nwill struggle to get anything more than a trace to a few \nhundredths of an inch.\n\nEarly next week is likely to be a transition period with weak\nupper level ridging briefly taking over and overall dry\nconditions. After a brief dip in temperatures on Saturday, daytime\nhighs should trend closer to normal for early next week. Looking\nout even further, ensemble guidance is hinting at another deep \nPacific trough moving into the Western U.S. early next week and\npotentially reaching our region during the middle part of next\nweek. This next weather system could bring more rain chances and\nanother cool down during the latter half of next week.",
